=== agent-deck connect: Codex CLI (name "codex", scopes read) ===
Token file: /home/me/.local/share/agent-deck/assistants/codex.token (0600). Revoke: agent-deck connect revoke codex

--- 1. Config: run the command, or merge the TOML into ~/.codex/config.toml ---
codex mcp add agentdeck -- /usr/local/bin/agent-deck connect serve --token-file /home/me/.local/share/agent-deck/assistants/codex.token

# or merge into ~/.codex/config.toml:
[mcp_servers.agentdeck]
command = "/usr/local/bin/agent-deck"
args = ["connect", "serve", "--token-file", "/home/me/.local/share/agent-deck/assistants/codex.token"]

--- 2. Test ---
codex mcp list
agent-deck connect test --name codex

--- 3. Guide: save the ready skill https://raw.githubusercontent.com/asheshgoplani/agent-deck/main/docs/assistants/skills/codex/SKILL.md as ~/.agents/skills/agent-deck/SKILL.md, or keep this text ---
You are connected to agent-deck on my machine as "codex" with scopes: read.
agent-deck is a terminal session manager for AI coding agents (Claude Code, Codex, Gemini, OpenCode and others): each session is one agent working in its own terminal. Through the agent-deck tools you can see those sessions and, when allowed, drive them.

Tools (MCP server "agentdeck"; the HTTP API at http://127.0.0.1:8420/api/assistant/v1 mirrors them):
- whoami: your name, scopes and limits
- list_sessions {group?, status?}: all sessions with id, title, tool, status, group, path (read)
- session_status {session}: one session's details (read)
- read_output {session}: the last answer of that session's agent (read)
- send_message {session, message}: type a message into a session (send)
- approve {session, choice?}: answer a visible approval prompt (approve)
- launch_session {path, title, tool?, message?}: start a new session (launch)

Rules:
1. Call list_sessions first and refer to sessions by id.
2. Read before acting: check session_status or read_output before you send or approve anything.
3. ASK THE USER and wait for a clear yes before send_message, approve or launch_session; show the exact text you will send.
4. Never stop, delete, restart or kill sessions. There is no tool for it; do not use a shell or any other way to do it.
5. Never read, print or change credentials, tokens or agent-deck's own files (~/.agent-deck, ~/.local/share/agent-deck).
6. If a tool answers "scope missing", do not work around it: tell the user the exact command from the answer (for example: agent-deck connect codex --scopes read,send).
7. Keep reports short: one line per session (title, status, what it is waiting for).

To allow more, run: agent-deck connect codex --scopes read,send,approve,launch (pick only the scopes you need; this rotates the token, stdio configs pick the new one up from the token file, HTTP configs need the new token)
=== end ===
